{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Web components · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Web components</h1>
    <p>
        php-via pages can use web components built with <a href="https://data-star.dev/reference/rocket" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rocket</a>,
        Datastar's component layer, such as the components in the
        <a href="https://starbase.zweiundeins.gmbh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Starbase</a> catalog.
        A Rocket component is a JavaScript module that imports Datastar, so the page needs a Datastar
        build that contains Rocket, and the component has to import the same copy of Datastar the page
        runs. php-via serves that build when you ask for it, and its default shell adds the import map
        that points the components at it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="enabling">Enabling the Rocket build</h2>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app = new Via(
    (new Config())
        ->withDatastarRocket()
);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>/datastar.js</code> then serves Datastar 1.0.4 with Rocket instead of the plain Datastar
        1.0.4 bundle. The build comes from Starbase: Datastar 1.0.4 and Rocket built with Starbase's
        fixes, which php-via ships because of the <a href="#keyed-lists">keyed-list crash</a> in the
        official Rocket bundle.
    </p>
    <p>
        The Rocket build is 21,958 bytes with Brotli, against 12,183 bytes for the plain bundle, and every
        page of the app loads it, whether the page uses a component or not. Leave the option off unless
        your pages use Rocket components.
    </p>
    <p>
        A reverse proxy that serves <code>/datastar.js</code> from disk has to serve
        <code>vendor/mbolli/php-via/public/datastar-rocket.js</code> instead, or pass the request on to
        php-via. With the plain bundle, every component module fails to load, because the bundle has no
        <code>rocket</code> export.
    </p>

    <h2 id="one-engine">One Datastar engine per page</h2>
    <p>
        Rocket components import Datastar by name, <code>import { rocket } from 'datastar'</code>. The
        browser resolves the name through the page's import map, which has to map <code>datastar</code>
        to exactly the URL the page loads Datastar from, query string included. A module is identified by
        its URL, so <code>/datastar.js</code> and <code>/datastar.js?v=f602fbe19d</code> are two modules.
        If the map and the script tag differ, the components import a second copy and start a second
        Datastar engine on the same page, with signals of its own. The import map also has to come before the first module script, so that it
        is in place when the first import resolves.
    </p>
    <p>
        With <code>withDatastarRocket()</code>, the default shell does both. It writes the map right after
        the viewport meta, ahead of everything <code>appendToHead()</code> adds, and loads Datastar from the
        same URL at the end of the body:
    </p>
    {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<script type="importmap">{"imports":{"datastar":"/datastar.js?v=f602fbe19d"}}</script>
...
<script type="module" src="/datastar.js?v=f602fbe19d"></script>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>v</code> is the first 10 hex digits of the served bundle's SHA-256, so a new bundle gets a
        new URL and browsers fetch it instead of using a cached copy. <code>Config::getDatastarUrl()</code>
        returns the URL, including the base path; in a page handler that is
        <code>$c-&gt;getConfig()-&gt;getDatastarUrl()</code>. With the plain bundle, the default shell
        loads Datastar from that URL and writes no import map.
    </p>
    <p id="csp">
        The import map is an inline script. Under a <code>Content-Security-Policy</code> whose
        <code>script-src</code> lacks <code>'unsafe-inline'</code>, the browser blocks it, and every
        component then fails to import <code>datastar</code>. Allow the map by its SHA-256, which changes
        with every bundle, or write it yourself with your nonce in a
        <a href="#own-shell">custom shell or Twig layout</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="own-shell">The import map in a custom shell or Twig layout</h2>
    <h3 id="custom-shell">Custom shell</h3>
    <p>
        A shell set with <code>withShellTemplate()</code> gets two placeholders:
        {% verbatim %}<code>{{ datastar_import_map }}</code>{% endverbatim %} is the whole import map tag
        (empty without <code>withDatastarRocket()</code>), and
        {% verbatim %}<code>{{ datastar_url }}</code>{% endverbatim %} is the URL. Put the map in the
        head, before {% verbatim %}<code>{{ head_content }}</code>{% endverbatim %} and any module script
        of your own:
    </p>
    {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    {{ datastar_import_map }}
    <meta data-signals='{{ signals_json }}'>
    <!-- the SSE bootstrap metas from the default shell.html -->
    {{ head_content }}
</head>
<body>
    {{ content }}
    {{ foot_content }}
    <script type="module" src="{{ datastar_url }}"></script>
</body>
</html>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Load Datastar from {% verbatim %}<code>{{ datastar_url }}</code>{% endverbatim %}. A shell that
        keeps {% verbatim %}<code>{{ base_path }}datastar.js</code>{% endverbatim %} loads Datastar from
        another URL than the map's, so the components start a second engine; php-via logs a warning
        when a shell has the map placeholder without the URL one. A shell without any import map works
        with the plain bundle but not with Rocket components, and an unversioned
        <code>datastar.js</code> stays cached for up to an hour after an upgrade.
    </p>

    <h3 id="twig-layout">Twig layout</h3>
    <p>
        php-via never adds the Datastar script or the import map to a view that renders its own
        <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> document (see <a href="/docs/twig#twig-shell">Twig as the shell</a>), so
        the layout loads Datastar from the <code>datastarUrl</code> variable and, with
        <code>withDatastarRocket()</code>, writes the import map for the same URL:
    </p>
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{# templates/base.html.twig #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    {# with withDatastarRocket() #}
    <script type="importmap">{"imports": {"datastar": "{{ datastarUrl }}"}}</script>
    <meta data-signals='{"via_ctx":"{{ contextId }}","_disconnected":false}'>
    <meta data-indicator="_connecting"
        data-on-interval__duration.15s.leading="!$_connecting && @get('{{ basePath }}_sse')">
    <meta data-init="window.addEventListener('beforeunload', () => { navigator.sendBeacon('{{ basePath }}_session/close', '{{ contextId }}'); });">
    <title>{% block title %}{% endblock %}</title>
</head>
<body>
    {% block content %}{% endblock %}
    <script type="module" src="{{ datastarUrl }}"></script>
</body>
</html>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The URL contains only letters, digits and <code>/ . _ - ? =</code>, so
        {% verbatim %}<code>{{ datastarUrl }}</code>{% endverbatim %} prints the same bytes inside the JSON
        and inside the attribute.
    </p>

    <h2 id="loading">Loading a Starbase component</h2>
    <p>
        Every Starbase component page has an Installation section with four tabs of snippets. Each snippet
        starts with an import map whose <code>imports</code> entry points <code>datastar</code> at
        Starbase's own copy of the build. Leave that entry out on a php-via page: php-via's map already
        points <code>datastar</code> at the build php-via serves.
    </p>

    <h3 id="catalog">From the catalog</h3>
    <p>
        The <em>This component</em> tab pins one component version: its minified module at an immutable
        URL, with an <code>integrity</code> hash, so the browser refuses the file if a single byte changes.
        With the default shell, add that tab's script tag with <code>appendToHead()</code>:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->appendToHead(
    '<script type="module" src="https://starbase.zweiundeins.gmbh/c/slider@<hash>/slider.min.js" integrity="sha384-<hash>"></script>',
);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The <code>integrity</code> attribute covers that one file. A component that imports files of its
        own, such as <code>sb-code-editor</code> with its vendored Prism, lists their hashes under
        <code>integrity</code> in the tab's import map. They have to go into the same map as the
        <code>datastar</code> entry, because a browser that supports one import map per page ignores a
        second one. Write that map yourself in a custom shell or layout, with php-via's URL:
    </p>
    {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<script type="importmap">
  {
    "imports": { "datastar": "{{ datastar_url }}" },
    "integrity": {
      "https://starbase.zweiundeins.gmbh/c/code-editor@<hash>/vendor/prism.min.js": "sha384-<hash>"
    }
  }
</script>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The <em>Autoloader</em> and <em>Pinned</em> tabs load Starbase's autoloader instead, which imports
        each <code>&lt;sb-*&gt;</code> component the first time its tag appears on the page, including
        tags a morph adds later. <code>/c/autoloader.js</code> always loads the latest version of each
        component. The <em>Pinned</em> tab's autoloader belongs to one snapshot of the catalog, and
        <code>/c/@&lt;catalog&gt;/importmap.json</code> holds the integrity hashes of every file that
        snapshot can load, for the import map above.
    </p>

    <h3 id="vendoring">Copied into your app</h3>
    <p>
        Components import only <code>datastar</code> and files from their own folder, so copying the
        folder is enough. Download the files the <em>Self-host</em> tab lists into your
        <code>withStaticDir()</code> directory, keeping each component's folder, and load the module from
        there:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withStaticDir(__DIR__ . '/public');  // public/js/slider/slider.min.js

$app->appendToHead('<script type="module" src="/js/slider/slider.min.js"></script>');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        php-via serves <code>.js</code> and <code>.mjs</code> files as JavaScript. The <em>Self-host</em>
        tab's import map points at a copy of <code>datastar-rocket.js</code> you would host yourself; leave
        it out, since php-via serves the build at <code>/datastar.js</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="binding">Binding signals to component properties</h2>
    <p>
        <code>$signal-&gt;bind('value')</code>, or {% verbatim %}<code>{{ bind(signal, 'value') }}</code>{% endverbatim %}
        in Twig, writes the property form of Datastar's <code>data-bind</code>, which binds the element's
        <code>value</code> property:
    </p>
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
<sb-slider label="Thrust" unit="%" {{ bind(thrust, 'value') }}></sb-slider>
{# renders data-bind__prop.value="<thrust's id>" #}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Use the property form for web components. Datastar then reads and writes the property, whether
        or not the module has defined the tag when Datastar reaches the element, and updates the signal
        on the element's <code>input</code> and <code>change</code> events. Plain <code>bind()</code>
        binds a custom element's <code>value</code> property only if its tag is already defined, and its
        <code>value</code> attribute otherwise, which a component does not have to keep up to date. Any
        other property, such as <code>checked</code> on <code>sb-toggle</code>, needs the property form.
        Starbase's docs bind every component this way.
    </p>
    <p>
        A camelCase name is written in kebab case, which Datastar turns back:
        <code>bind('selectedIndex')</code> gives <code>data-bind__prop.selected-index</code>. For further
        modifiers, such as the <code>__event.change</code> that Starbase's docs use on toggles, write the
        attribute with the signal's id:
    </p>
    {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<sb-toggle label="Thrusters" data-bind__prop.checked__event.change="{{ thrusters.id }}"></sb-toggle>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The usual rules for client values apply: a TAB signal takes the value the browser posts with the
        next action, and a scoped signal ignores it unless it is declared <code>clientWritable: true</code>
        (see <a href="/docs/api#context-signal">signal()</a>).
    </p>

    <h2 id="server-state">Keeping state on the server</h2>
    <p>
        A component holds what only the browser needs: whether a menu is open, where the focus is, a drag
        in progress. Everything else stays in php-via. The view renders the server's values into the
        component's attributes, every <code>sync()</code> and broadcast renders them again, and the
        component reports a change with an event that posts to an action. The action decides what the
        value becomes:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->page('/thrust', function (Context $c): void {
    $thrust = $c->signal(40, 'thrust');

    $c->action(function (Context $c) use ($thrust): void {
        $thrust->setValue(min($thrust->int(), 80));
        $c->sync();
    }, 'setThrust');

    $c->view('thrust.html.twig');
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{# thrust.html.twig #}
<div id="thrust">
    <sb-slider label="Thrust" unit="%" value="{{ thrust.int }}" {{ bind(thrust, 'value') }}
        data-on:change="@post('{{ setThrust.url }}')"></sb-slider>
    <p>The server holds {{ thrust.int }}.</p>
</div>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>sb-slider</code> updates the bound signal while it is dragged and fires <code>change</code>
        when the user lets go. The post carries the signal, the action caps it at 80 and syncs, and the
        thumb moves back to 80. Starbase's form components take a new <code>value</code> attribute from
        the server as their value, and keep the user's edit when the server sends the same markup again,
        so a re-render for an unrelated reason does not reset them. Every commit posts to the same URL,
        and each post cancels the one before it if it is still in flight, which suits a slider: the last
        post carries the latest value. With <code>confirm</code>, Starbase's value components also show a
        value as pending until the server's attribute matches it, and <code>revert()</code> goes back to
        the server's value; Starbase's
        <a href="https://starbase.zweiundeins.gmbh/contribute#commands-and-components" target="_blank" rel="noopener">commands and components</a>
        guide describes the contract.
    </p>

    <h2 id="keyed-lists">Rocket components inside keyed lists</h2>
    <p>
        Datastar's morph matches elements by <code>id</code>, so a list whose items carry ids keeps its
        elements when a sync or broadcast reorders it. With the official Datastar 1.0.4 Rocket bundle, a
        morph that reorders keyed elements containing a Rocket component overflows the stack
        (<code>Maximum call stack size exceeded</code>). When the same patch also inserts or removes
        elements, a <code>HierarchyRequestError</code> stops the morph halfway and leaves the page out of
        step with the server. Two of Starbase's patches, 0002 and 0008, fix it (upstream issues
        <a href="https://github.com/starfederation/datastar/issues/1218" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#1218</a> and
        <a href="https://github.com/starfederation/datastar/issues/1209" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#1209</a>),
        which is why <code>withDatastarRocket()</code> serves Starbase's build.
        <a href="https://github.com/mbolli/php-via/blob/master/public/DATASTAR.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">public/DATASTAR.md</a>
        lists all twelve patches, the source commits and the hashes the test suite checks.
    </p>

    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/twig">Templates</a>: shells, Twig layouts and binding</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/actions#cancellation">Actions</a>: requests that cancel each other</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/api#config">API reference</a>: <code>withDatastarRocket()</code> and <code>getDatastarUrl()</code></li>
        <li><a href="https://starbase.zweiundeins.gmbh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Starbase</a>: the component catalog</li>
    </ul>
</article>
{% endblock %}
